{"name":"Timberwolf","hex":"#b4aca3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"timberwolf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of timber wolf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/timberwolf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Subspecies of Canis lupus","blocks":[{"text":"There are 38 subspecies of Canis lupus listed in the taxonomic authority Mammal Species of the World. These subspecies were named over the past 250 years, and since their naming, a number of them have gone extinct. The nominate subspecies is the Eurasian wolf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/The_Wolves_of_North_America_%281944%29_C._lupus_subspecies_skulls.jpg/330px-The_Wolves_of_North_America_%281944%29_C._lupus_subspecies_skulls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subspecies_of_Canis_lupus","wikidata":"Q3076098"},{"title":"Eastern wolf","blocks":[{"text":"The eastern wolf, also known as the timber wolf, Algonquin wolf and eastern timber wolf, is a canine of debated taxonomy native to the Great Lakes region and southeastern Canada. It is considered either a unique subspecies of gray wolf, or red wolf, or a separate species from both. Many studies have found the eastern wolf to be the product of ancient and recent genetic admixture between the gray wolf and the coyote, while other studies have found some or all populations of the eastern wolf, as well as coyotes, originally separated from a common ancestor with the wolf over 1 million years ago and that these populations of the eastern wolf may be the same species as or a closely related species to the red wolf of the Southeastern United States. Regardless of its status, it is regarded as unique and therefore worthy of conservation with Canada citing the population in eastern Canada as being the eastern wolf population subject to protection."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Eastern_wolf_in_Algonquin_Provincial_Park_01.jpg/330px-Eastern_wolf_in_Algonquin_Provincial_Park_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_wolf","wikidata":"Q677746"},{"title":"Timberwolf (web browser)","blocks":[{"text":"Timberwolf was a port of the Firefox web browser to the AmigaOS 4 platform."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7b/Timberwolf_Alpha_1.png/330px-Timberwolf_Alpha_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timberwolf_(web_browser)","wikidata":"Q2394363"},{"title":"List of Crayola crayon colors","blocks":[{"text":"Since the introduction of Crayola drawing crayons by Binney & Smith in 1903, more than 200 colors have been produced in a wide variety of assortments. The table below represents all of the colors found in regular Crayola assortments from 1903 to the present. Since the introduction of fluorescent crayons in the 1970s, the standard colors have been complemented by a number of specialty crayon assortments, represented in subsequent tables."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Crayola1.jpg/330px-Crayola1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Crayola_crayon_colors","wikidata":"Q6568034"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Original_distribution_of_gray_wolf_%28canis_lupus%29_subspecies-en.png/500px-Original_distribution_of_gray_wolf_%28canis_lupus%29_subspecies-en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Original_distribution_of_gray_wolf_%28canis_lupus%29_subspecies-en.png/1280px-Original_distribution_of_gray_wolf_%28canis_lupus%29_subspecies-en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn